> ghudson@cie.UUCP (Greg Hudson) writes:
>
>     I just thought I'd mention some things about the idea of a
>water_confirm option.  One of the biggest disadvantages would
>indoubtedly be the "Omega effect" (Omega has such an option) whereby you
>have to hit "y" each time you enter the pool (I don't think typing "yes"
>and return is warranted.  People fall in pools so rarely that the chance
>of accidentally stepping on a pool and having your modem send "y" at
>that moment is miniscule.  Sometimes airtightness isn't worth the
>inconvenience).

I don't think that people will want to *enter* pools very often (see
your suggestion below).  I presume that nethack has only one
confirmation routine, which is used regardless of circumstances.  I
think that "yes" is important wrt the Q command at least because of
the similarity between qy and QY.  There was also a posting a while
back from someone who typed yyyyy when taking stuff out of a bag (all
because of the strange -- and unpopular -- implementation of "a").
There were only three things in the bag and a priest to the northwest.
The last two y's attacked the priest and confirmed the attack.  This
could just as easily drown you.  I don't think I proposed that you
type "yes" each time you want to remove an object for a bag; that
would be tedious.  Of course there will always be someone who says
that winners *respect* bags and all players should be cautious around
them, because that is in the spirit of the game.  Keeps them on their
toes.  :-)

Seriously whether to type "y" or "yes" is a general issue that could
be resolved satisfactorily in many ways; flushing typeahead before
confirmation is a fairly innocuous partial fix.  Whether to confirm
entering water is best decided separately.

>     It seems to me that checking for levitation and identified boots of
>...lets the game play for the character too much.

I guess I'll agree with that.

> However, one *could*
>make it so there is no confirm if the character was *previously* on a
>water square.  Thus one would only have to confirm once each time he
>steps into the water.

Awesome.  I hope you get the go-ahead to code it.

>
>     Just a side-gripe: I note that some items like "leather shoes" can
>rust.  ("Your metal boots rust!")  Likewise, "a black cap" doesn't
>strike me as being made of metal.

Yeah.  The "fake" names are a bit too inaccurate in places.  This is
complicated by the fact that some of them are random.  Ideally, you
should be allowed an accurate impression of what the armor is made of.

